<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Southern Maratha Country</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Southern Maratha Country States</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> Country was the British name for the region between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> and Tungabhadra rivers, covering parts of modern southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>. After the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshwa">Peshwa</a> in 1818 its Maratha jagirdars, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patwardhan">Patwardhans</a> of Sangli, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miraj">Miraj</a>, Jamkhandi and Kurundwad, became small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a> under British protection.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/southern-maratha-country.jpg" alt="Southern Maratha Country" width="320" height="454" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: by PlaneMad; derivative work by Nayvik · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Belagavi_in_Karnataka_(India).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
